findings.body:
`prefect_test_harness` only accepts `server_startup_timeout` (src/prefect/testing/utilities.py:114). The conftest call passes `server_timeout=60`, so it raises `TypeError: unexpected keyword argument` as soon as the session-scoped, autouse `prefect_db` fixture runs. That fixture runs for every test in the package, so the entire prefect-gcp suite errors before any test executes. This also means the CI timeout fix in the PR description is never actually applied.

::diff{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/tests/conftest.py" lines="13-21"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The intent (a longer startup timeout for CI) is good. Only the keyword name is wrong.

::suggestion{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/tests/conftest.py" lines="19-19"}
    with prefect_test_harness(server_startup_timeout=60):

The guard is `if self.bucket_folder and self.bucket_folder in path: return path`. This is a substring test, not a prefix test, and the PR description and the existing `_join_bucket_folder` (L928, `startswith`) both assume a prefix check. With `bucket_folder="results/"`, these keys are all returned unchanged and written to the bucket root instead of under `results/`:

- `my-results/abc123` (contains `results/`)
- `data/results/x`
- `archive/results/2024/run.json`

The two code paths now disagree about the same input, and the error is silent: the object lands in the wrong place with no exception. Real Prefect result keys are usually hashes, so the impact is low in the common case. User-supplied paths passed to `write_path`, `read_path`, or `get_directory` can still hit it.

::diff{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/prefect_gcp/cloud_storage.py" lines="726-736"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The fix for the double-nesting bug only needs to detect a leading folder prefix. Use a prefix check.

::suggestion{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/prefect_gcp/cloud_storage.py" lines="734-735"}
        if self.bucket_folder and path.startswith(self.bucket_folder):
            return path

The conftest diff deletes the session-wide `disable_logging` fixture, which set `PREFECT_LOGGING_TO_API_ENABLED: False` through `temporary_settings`. It also removes the `temporary_settings` and `PREFECT_LOGGING_TO_API_ENABLED` imports. The PR description says only the harness timeout was changed, so this removal is unexplained. Without it, log records from the prefect-gcp test run are sent to the test API server, which can make tests slower or noisier and can change assertions about logging.

::diff{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/tests/conftest.py" lines="1-21"}

If the removal was intentional (for example, the setting is now the default in test mode), say so in the PR and the commit message. Otherwise restore the fixture next to `prefect_db`.

`model_validator` is added to the pydantic import, but the only other mention in the file is in a comment. Nothing uses the import. Ruff's default rule set (F401) flags it, so this will fail the project's lint check. It is a leftover from the cross-field validator that was written as a `field_validator`.

::diff{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/prefect_gcp/cloud_storage.py" lines="10-12"}

Remove `model_validator` from the import, or use it if the validator is moved as suggested in the previous finding.

The `pyproject.toml` change replaces the existing `google._upb` `PyType_Spec` deprecation filter rather than adding to it. The PR description says filters were added for httplib2, pathspec, google.api_core, and coolname. The diff only adds httplib2 and pathspec. The google.api_core and coolname filters are not present, and the removed `google._upb` filter is not mentioned. Either the description is out of date, or the diff is incomplete, or the removal is accidental.

::diff{file="src/integrations/prefect-gcp/pyproject.toml" lines="103-106"}

Keep the existing filter and append the new ones, then update the PR description to match what is actually in the diff.
